      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Cutaneous burns are a common cause of morbidity following direct current (DC) cardioversion. We designed a prospective double-blinded controlled study to determine whether the application of steroid cream prior to cardioversion reduces their incidence and severity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Two hours before elective DC cardioversion, we applied betamethasone 0.1% cream or placebo cream over sternal and apical pad sites in 56 patients, with patients acting as their own controls. Two hours after cardioversion, a separate blinded observer measured the visual analogue pain score (VAS), sensory and pain detection thresholds, skin temperature and erythema index at sternal and apical pad sites."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The study had an 80% power to detect a 50% difference in VAS at 2 h, accepting an alpha error of 0.05. There was no difference between pain at 2 or 24 h, skin temperature, erythema index, sensory and pain detection thresholds at pad sites treated with steroid cream or control."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Topical betamethasone 0.1% cream applied 2 h before elective DC cardioversion is no more effective than placebo at reducing the pain and inflammation from cardioversion burns."
        }
      ]},
